`onDidChangeSemanticTokens` is an invalidation hint, not a guaranteed immediate provider invocation. VS Code coalesces semantic-token requests, and an event fired while it already has a request in flight can be effectively absorbed. Your command working later is consistent with that timing race.

Make the provider itself version-aware: when VS Code asks for tokens for document version `N`, wait until your server has processed version `N` (honouring the cancellation token), then return tokens for that same version. Don’t rely on a second event to repair a request that returned stale data.

```ts
class SemanticProvider implements vscode.DocumentSemanticTokensProvider {
  private readonly changed = new vscode.EventEmitter<void>();

  readonly onDidChangeSemanticTokens = this.changed.event;

  async provideDocumentSemanticTokens(
    document: vscode.TextDocument,
    token: vscode.CancellationToken,
  ): Promise<vscode.SemanticTokens | null> {
    await server.whenParsed(document.uri, document.version, token);
    return token.isCancellationRequested
      ? null
      : server.tokensFor(document.uri, document.version);
  }

  invalidate(): void {
    this.changed.fire();
  }
}
```

Also simplify and correct the event subscription API. `Event.call(...)` happens to work here, but it is obscure and discards the `Disposable`:

```ts
registerOnSentTextEdits(
  callback: () => void,
  thisArg?: unknown,
): vscode.Disposable {
  return this.mOnSentTextEditsEvent(callback, thisArg);
}
```

Keep that disposable and dispose it with the provider/extension.

There is another race in the shown queue code: every text change attaches a `.then()` to `mQueuedNotification`, while later changes replace that promise. An earlier completion can clear `mTextEdits` or set `mQueuedNotification = null` after a newer batch was installed. Associate each batch with a monotonically increasing generation/document version, and only clear state if the completing batch is still current.

Finally, VS Code 1.71.2 is very old. Upgrade before treating this as a product bug—but even on current versions, design semantic-token provision as a pull request for a specific document version, not as a push-refresh guarantee. The API defines the event as optional provider change notification; VS Code owns when it requests tokens. [VS Code Extension API](https://code.visualstudio.com/api/references/vscode-api#DocumentSemanticTokensProvider)
